The following are functions available in cnetcdoor.lib and are available
for CNetC door use.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CallHost
ProtoType: void CallHost( UBYTE commandnum )
Purpose  : To invoke a command internal to the BBS.  This causes "BBS" to
           execute an internal version of the functions listed below.
           CallHost() is the core of the CNetC built-in routines. CallHost
           is generally not invoked by the CNetC door but is used internally
           by the C#? functions below.
Arguments: Number of internal command to invoke.
Returns  : none.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CAddCharge
ProtoType: UBYTE CAddCharge( short schedulenum, short amount )
Purpose  : To add a charge to a user's CNet schedule.  Is applied to the
           account of the user who is on the port which the CNetC door
           is executed.
Arguments: schedulenum = number of the schedule to which this charge is added.
           amount = the amount to add to the account.  negative values may be
           used for subtraction of an amount.
Returns  : FALSE (0) if charge could not be added.
           TRUE  (1) if charge added successfully
Bugs     : non reported.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CCallEditor
ProtoType: void CCallEditor( short max, short inlines )
Purpose  : Invokes the CNet editor according to user's preference of line or Visual
Arguments: max = Number of lines of editor space to allow user.
           inlines = if set to TRUE (1), the editor will import, and allow
                     the user to edit the text existant in _edbuffX
                     where X is the user's port number.
                     if set to FALSE (0), CNet opens the editor with no text
                     present.
Returns  : none.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CCheckBalance
ProtoType: UBYTE CCheckBalance( short schedule, short scalefactor )
Purpose  : Companion function to AddCharge().  Checks if the users balance
           would allow the addition of a charge.
Arguments: charge = the number of the charge to be applied. 
           scalefactor = the factor to be applied to the charge default value
           before checking the user's balance.

Example  : CCheckBalance(4, 2);
           This would cause CNet to compare twice the amount of charge number
           4 with the user's balance and return the result.
Returns  : FALSE (0) - means the user's account cannot handle this charge
                       without being forced into a negative situation.
           TRUE (1) - mean this charge can be applied and the user's balance
                      would still be positive.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CCheckChanges
ProtoType: void CCheckChanges( void )
Purpose  : Force CNet to update the current time, load the user's preferred
           bbstext translation, charges If the user has little time remaining,
           print "you have xx minutes left" and force Events to be checked for
           pending execution.
Arguments: none.
Returns  : none.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CCheckFlowControl
ProtoType: void CCheckFlowControl( void )
Purpose  : Forces CNet to flush/check it's buffers and parse/update the
           internal keyboard buffers.
Arguments: none.
Returns  : none.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CCheckOLM
ProtoType: char CCheckOLM( void )
Purpose  : Check if any OLMs are waiting and display them if so.
Arguments: none.
Returns  : 0 if no OLMs are waiting.
           1 if OLMs were waiting and have been displayed.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CCommonCommands
ProtoType: long CCommonCommands( void )
Purpose  : Check z->InBuffer for Chat, OLM, etc. and execute any "everywhere
           commands" that match.
Arguments: This function expects the command and its arguments to be
           setup in z->InBuffer.
Example  :
           strcpy(z->InBuffer, "MR");
           CommonCommands();

Returns  : 0 if no commands were executed.
           1 if some "everywhere command" was executed.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CConvertAccess
ProtoType: long CConvertAccess( char *s )
Purpose  : Converts a CNet access/range string to a packed LONG value,
Arguments: String containing a range of flags in CNet form. eg. 1,4,5-9
Returns  : bit representation of the flags in packed LONG form.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CDoANSI
ProtoType: void CDoANSI( UBYTE cmd, USHORT one, USHORT two)
Purpose  : Print the specified ANSI sequence.
Arguments: cmd = the ANSI command to execute. eg "H"
           one = the first argument to the ANSI sequence.
           two = the second argument to the ANSI sequence.
Example  :
           CDoANSI("H", 3, 5);

           The above example would invoke the ANSI "H" command and cause the
           cursor to go to line 3, column 5.

Returns  : none.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function : CDoANSIOut
ProtoType: void CDoANSIOut( UBYTE n)
Purpose  : Invoke an ANSI command that requires no arguments.
Arguments: n = a signelcharacter representing the ANSI command.
Example  :
           CDoANSIOut('s');

           The above command would cause CNet to save the current cursor
           position internally.
 
Returns  : none.
Bugs     : none.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Function         : CPutQMult
ProtoType        : UBYTE CPutQMult( char *prompt, char *possiblities)
Purpose          : This function operates similar to the YES or NO "CPutQ()"
                   function.  Both of these functions print a prompt and return
                   a value based on the key pressed by the user.  CPutQMult()
                   differs from CPutQ in that CPutQMult allows more than 2
                   keypress possibilities.  Where CPutQ allows Y, N or ENTER,
                   CPutQMulti() lets the coder and, if coded properly, the user
                   choose what keys to press and allows more than 2 choices.
                   Based on the given choices, CPutQMult() returns a value
                   equivalent to the key pressed.
Arguments        : prompt = the string to print before accepting user input.
                   possbilities = a string of keys available to the user
                   and, as an option, a default key represented by the ENTER/RETURN
                   keys. eg. "ABFGT G" where the final 'G' is the default key for
                   and ENTER keypress.  Note:  If the default character is not
                   specified in the possbilities string, the FIRST character of
                   the possibilities string is used as a default for RETURN/ENTER
                   keys.  eg. in the possbilities string "WYNQ", 'W' would be used
                   as the default.
link requirements: Global pointer to CNet port data (struct PortData *z)
Returns          : positional value of the key pressed as represented by
                   the "possibliities" input string.
Example          : value = CPutQMult("Press A, B, T, or Q [ENTER=T]", "ABTQ T")

                         Key pressed return value
                         ----------- ------------                    
                              A            1
                              B            2
                              T            3
                              Q            4
                            ENTER          3
                            RETURN         3
Bugs             : none.

